__d(function(e,t,a,o,n,s,i){n.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"English",textbook:"A Pact with the Sun",chapter_number:2,chapter_title:"The Friendly Mongoose",estimated_study_time_minutes:45,difficulty:"Easy",learning_objectives:["Understand the plot and characters of 'The Friendly Mongoose'","Identify the theme: acting in haste leads to regret","Learn and use new vocabulary from the story in context","Understand the relationship between the farmer, his wife, child, and the mongoose","State the moral of the story and relate it to real-life situations","Distinguish between a hasty decision and a thoughtful decision","Answer comprehension questions based on the story"],prerequisite_knowledge:["Basic reading comprehension from Class 5","Understanding of simple fables and morals","Knowledge of what a mongoose is (a small animal known for fighting snakes)"],introduction:{why_important:"This story teaches a timeless lesson: never act in haste. Making quick decisions without thinking leads to regret. This is one of the most important life skills a person can develop.",real_life_relevance:"Every day, people make quick decisions without thinking \u2014 punishing someone without hearing both sides, spreading a rumour without checking, breaking a friendship over a misunderstanding. This story warns us against all such hasty actions.",karnataka_context:"Mongooses are well-known in Karnataka's rural areas. In many villages, mongooses are kept as pets because they protect homes from snakes. Children in Karnataka grow up hearing stories about the loyal mongoose. The Panchatantra \u2014 written in ancient India \u2014 has many stories with a similar moral about hasty decisions.",connection_to_previous_learning:"In Chapter 1, we learned how environment shapes character. In this chapter, we learn about the consequences of hasty action \u2014 showing that good intentions are not enough; we also need to think before we act."},story_summary:{characters:[{name:"The Farmer",role:"A kind man who keeps a mongoose as a pet and loves both his child and the mongoose"},{name:"The Farmer's Wife",role:"The mother of the baby; she acts hastily when she sees blood on the mongoose"},{name:"The Baby",role:"The farmer's infant child, left at home while the parents go out"},{name:"The Mongoose",role:"A loyal, friendly pet that saves the baby from a deadly snake"},{name:"The Snake",role:"A cobra that enters the house and threatens the baby"}],plot:["A farmer and his wife have a baby. They also keep a mongoose as a pet.","The farmer's wife does not fully trust the mongoose near the baby.","One day, the farmer goes to the field. The wife needs to fetch water from the well.","She is worried about leaving the baby alone but cannot take both the baby and the water pot.","She leaves the baby at home with the mongoose and goes to fetch water.","While she is gone, a cobra enters the house and approaches the baby's cradle.","The brave mongoose fights the cobra and kills it, saving the baby's life.","Proud of its deed, the mongoose runs to the doorstep to greet the returning mother, its mouth covered in blood.","The wife sees blood on the mongoose's mouth and assumes it has killed her baby.","Without checking, she throws her heavy water pot at the mongoose and kills it.","She rushes inside and finds the baby safe and a dead cobra on the floor.","She realizes her terrible mistake \u2014 the mongoose was a hero, not a killer.","She weeps with grief and regret, but it is too late.","Moral: Never act in haste. Always think before you act."],moral:"Hasty action leads to regret. Never act without thinking. A moment's thought can prevent a lifetime of sorrow."},core_concepts:[{concept_name:"The danger of hasty decisions",simple_explanation:"Acting quickly without thinking causes mistakes that cannot be undone.",detailed_explanation:"The farmer's wife saw blood on the mongoose and immediately assumed the worst. She did not check on the baby first. She did not look around the room. She acted in one instant \u2014 and that instant cost the mongoose its life. The story shows that quick, unthought-through actions cause irreversible harm. 'Hasty' means doing something very quickly without taking time to think.",real_life_example:"A teacher punishes a student for making noise in class without finding out that the student was telling the neighbour to stop disturbing. Acting without knowing all the facts causes injustice.",karnataka_example:"A Kannada proverb says: 'Alochaneginta munche kaaryakke ilidare, pachhaattaapa paaduvudu' \u2014 Acting before thinking leads to regret. Elders in Karnataka often warn young people against making snap decisions.",common_mistakes:"Students sometimes blame the mongoose for going to greet the mother with a bloody mouth. But the mongoose was an animal acting naturally \u2014 it could not know the mother would misunderstand. The fault lies entirely with the hasty action of the wife.",memory_tip:"Think \u2192 Act. Never Act \u2192 Think. Reverse the order and you get the farmer's wife's tragedy."},{concept_name:"Loyalty and sacrifice",simple_explanation:"The mongoose risked its own life to protect the baby, showing deep loyalty.",detailed_explanation:"The mongoose fought a deadly cobra to protect a baby that was not even its own species. This is a powerful act of loyalty \u2014 staying true to those who have given you love and shelter, even at great personal risk. Sadly, the mongoose's loyalty was rewarded with death, because of misunderstanding. The story makes us appreciate those who are truly loyal to us and warns us not to misjudge them.",real_life_example:"A friend who stays up all night helping you study before an exam, or a dog that barks to warn its family of a fire, shows similar loyalty.",karnataka_example:"Dogs and mongooses kept in Karnataka villages are known for their fierce loyalty to their families. Stories of dogs saving children from snakes are not uncommon in rural Karnataka.",common_mistakes:"Students sometimes feel sorry for the wife. While her grief is understandable, the story does not excuse her action. The lesson is about her mistake, not about comforting her.",memory_tip:"Mongoose = Loyal hero. Mother = Hasty mistake. Baby = Safe. Mongoose = Dead. Lesson = Think first."},{concept_name:"Consequences of actions",simple_explanation:"Every action has a result. Some results cannot be reversed.",detailed_explanation:"The farmer's wife's action had a permanent, irreversible consequence. The mongoose was dead \u2014 it could not be brought back. This teaches us about the permanence of certain actions: words once spoken, blows once given, lives once taken \u2014 these cannot be undone. This is why thinking before acting is not just wise but morally necessary.",real_life_example:"Sending a hurtful message, breaking a friend's trust, or cheating in an exam \u2014 all have consequences that are difficult or impossible to reverse.",karnataka_example:"The story mirrors lessons from the Panchatantra tale 'The Brahmin and the Mongoose' \u2014 one of the most well-known stories in Indian literature, teaching the same lesson across thousands of years.",common_mistakes:"Students sometimes think the moral is 'don't keep pets.' That is NOT the moral. The moral is specifically about hasty decisions \u2014 not about pet-keeping.",memory_tip:"Consequence = permanent result of action. Think of an arrow once shot \u2014 it cannot come back."}],key_terms_glossary:[{term:"Mongoose",meaning:"A small, quick animal known for its ability to fight and kill snakes; often kept as a pet in Indian villages"},{term:"Hasty",meaning:"Done too quickly, without thinking carefully"},{term:"Regret",meaning:"A feeling of sadness or sorrow about something you did or failed to do"},{term:"Loyal",meaning:"Always supporting and being faithful to someone, even in difficult times"},{term:"Cobra",meaning:"A large, venomous (poisonous) snake found in India; its bite can be deadly"},{term:"Cradle",meaning:"A small bed for a baby, usually one that rocks"},{term:"Consequence",meaning:"The result or effect of an action"},{term:"Irreversible",meaning:"Something that cannot be undone or changed back"},{term:"Assumption",meaning:"A belief that something is true without checking or proving it"},{term:"Grief",meaning:"Deep sadness, usually caused by loss"}],worked_examples:[{example_number:1,type:"Comprehension",question:"Why did the farmer's wife leave the baby alone at home?",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: The wife needed to fetch water from the well.","Step 2: She could not take the baby with her because she was carrying a heavy water pot.","Step 3: She trusted the mongoose to stay near the baby.","Step 4: She left the baby at home with the mongoose and went to the well.","Conclusion: She left the baby alone out of necessity, trusting the mongoose to keep watch."]},{example_number:2,type:"Vocabulary in context",question:"Use the word 'hasty' in a sentence connected to the story.",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Recall the meaning \u2014 hasty means acting too quickly without thinking.","Step 2: Identify the hasty action in the story \u2014 the wife killed the mongoose without checking on the baby first.","Step 3: Write sentence: 'The farmer's wife made a hasty decision when she threw the pot at the mongoose without first checking whether her baby was safe.'"]},{example_number:3,type:"Moral application",question:"How can the moral of this story help you in school life?",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: State the moral \u2014 never act in haste; think before you act.","Step 2: Think of a school situation \u2014 a classmate accuses another student of taking their pen.","Step 3: Apply it \u2014 before accusing someone, check your bag again, ask calmly, and think before reacting angrily.","Step 4: Conclusion: Thinking first prevents hurting innocent people and damaging friendships."]}],quick_check_questions:[{after_concept:"The danger of hasty decisions",questions:[{type:"Recall",q:"What did the mongoose do while the wife was away?",a:"It fought and killed a cobra that had entered the house to harm the baby."},{type:"Recall",q:"What did the wife see when she returned?",a:"She saw blood on the mongoose's mouth/face."},{type:"Understanding",q:"Why did the wife kill the mongoose?",a:"She assumed, without checking, that the mongoose had killed her baby because its mouth was covered in blood."},{type:"Understanding",q:"What mistake did the wife make?",a:"She acted hastily \u2014 she killed the mongoose without first going inside to check on her baby."},{type:"Application",q:"What should the wife have done instead of throwing the pot? How would things have been different?",a:"She should have first gone inside to check on the baby. She would have seen the baby safe and the dead cobra, and understood that the mongoose was the hero, not the killer. The mongoose would have been spared."}]}],key_takeaways:["A farmer and his wife keep a mongoose as a pet alongside their baby.","A cobra enters the house and is killed by the loyal mongoose.","The wife sees blood on the mongoose and hastily kills it, thinking it harmed the baby.","She finds the baby safe and a dead cobra \u2014 she realizes her terrible mistake.","The moral: Never act in haste. Think before you act.","Hasty actions cause irreversible harm and deep regret.","Loyalty should be recognized and appreciated, not punished through misunderstanding.","This story is similar to the Panchatantra tale of the Brahmin and the Mongoose."],mind_map:{root:"The Friendly Mongoose",branches:[{branch:"Characters",sub:["Farmer","Farmer's Wife","Baby","Mongoose (hero)","Cobra (villain)"]},{branch:"Plot",sub:["Baby left with mongoose","Cobra enters house","Mongoose kills cobra","Wife sees blood","Wife kills mongoose hastily","Baby found safe","Wife regrets"]},{branch:"Theme",sub:["Hasty action = regret","Loyalty rewarded with death","Think before you act"]},{branch:"Moral",sub:["Never act in haste","Check facts before deciding","Irreversible consequences"]},{branch:"Literary connections",sub:["Fable genre","Panchatantra parallel","Indian storytelling tradition"]}]},revision_notes:"The Friendly Mongoose is a fable about a loyal mongoose that saves a farmer's baby from a deadly cobra. The mother, seeing blood on the mongoose's mouth when she returns, assumes it has killed the baby and throws her water pot at it, killing it instantly. She rushes inside and finds the baby safe and a dead cobra \u2014 she realizes the mongoose was a hero. The moral is: never act in haste; hasty actions lead to irreversible regret. Key vocabulary: mongoose, hasty, regret, loyal, cobra, consequence, irreversible, assumption. Remember: Think \u2192 Act, never Act \u2192 Think.",must_remember_facts:["The mongoose is a pet kept by the farmer and his wife.","The wife is worried about leaving the baby alone.","She leaves the baby with the mongoose and goes to fetch water.","A cobra enters the house and the mongoose kills it to protect the baby.","The wife sees blood on the mongoose's mouth and assumes it killed the baby.","She throws her water pot at the mongoose and kills it \u2014 without checking on the baby first.","Inside, she finds the baby safe and a dead cobra.","The mongoose was the hero \u2014 it gave its life to protect the baby.","The moral: Never act in haste. Think before you act.","This story is similar to the famous Panchatantra tale 'The Brahmin and the Mongoose'."]}},2571,[]);